From 8d89b0ad304def06fc2483dc9232e40da7d534da Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Deniz Kokden <koekdendeniz@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 2 Nov 2026 15:26:26 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 066/109] media: i2c: add SiliconFile SR200PC20M sensor driver

The SR200PC20M is a 2 MP sensor with its own ISP and a one-lane MIPI
CSI-2 output delivering UYVY. It is the front camera of the Samsung
Galaxy Tab E 9.6 (SM-T560).

The sensor is configured entirely through vendor register tables (8-bit
registers in pages); no public documentation exists. The driver loads
them as firmware: sr200pc20m/init.bin followed by one table per mode,
each a list of little-endian {u16 reg, u16 val} pairs, with reg 0xff
meaning a delay of val * 10 ms. Modes: 800x600 (96 MHz link), 640x480
(144 MHz, self-contained table) and 1600x1200 (192 MHz).

The tables end by starting the stream, so the driver puts the sensor to
sleep (page 0, reg 0x01 = 0x31) after writing them and wakes it
(0x30) in enable_streams: in between the lane sits in LP-11, which the
receiver needs to bring up its D-PHY (pre_streamon with
V4L2_SUBDEV_PRE_STREAMON_FL_MANUAL_LP).

Power sequence as the vendor kernel: supplies, powerdown released,
MCLK (24 MHz), 30 ms, reset released, 10 ms. Probe checks the chip ID
(0xb4 at page 0, reg 0x04).

Tested on the Galaxy Tab E 9.6 with the SC7730 DCAM driver: all three
modes stream (640x480 at 24 fps, 800x600 at 11.5 fps, 1600x1200 at
8.8 fps indoors), runtime PM powers the sensor down after streaming.

Assisted-by: Claude Opus 5.5
Signed-off-by: Deniz Kokden <koekdendeniz@gmail.com>
---
